     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "A patient has dual insurance coverage through both parents' employer plans. The patient is a 12-year-old dependent. Parent A's birthday is October 3, Parent B's birthday is April 18. Under the NAIC Birthday Rule, which plan is primary?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "Parent A's plan is primary because Parent A is listed first on the enrollment form",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Parent B's plan is primary because Parent B's birthday falls earlier in the calendar year",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "The plan with the higher premium is always primary for dependents",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "The parent who has had coverage longer has the primary plan",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "The NAIC Birthday Rule determines primary coverage for dependent children by comparing parents' birthdays within the calendar year -- not by age or who is older. Parent B's birthday (April 18) falls earlier in the calendar year than Parent A's (October 3), making Parent B's plan primary. This rule only applies to dependent children; for the parents themselves, their own employer plan is always primary over a spouse's plan.",
